<p>
I record brief introductions for all the <a href="https://emacsconf.org/organizers-notebook/">EmacsConf</a> talks so that they can be played automatically. That way, hosts don't have to worry about pronunciations on air, and there's one less changeover that might introduce technical issues. We can cut to the next talk a bit abruptly because everything runs off <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2023/10/emacsconf-backstage-autopilot-with-crontab/">a crontab-powered schedule</a> to keep things running on time on multiple tracks, so it helps to have a little transition between Q&amp;A and the next talk. It's also nice having some kind of pronunciation reference so that when people discuss the talks or even just think about them with their internal monologues, they might have a better sense of how to say the speakers' names. We ask speakers to say their names at the beginning of their presentation, but people don't often include their last names, so the intro gets that consistently. The intro videos are available on the talk pages after the conference, and I've occasionally used them to remember how someone's name is pronounced.
</p>

<p>
Here's an example intro from EmacsConf 2025:
