<p>
The text is based on the Org entry properties for each talk in our conf.org file.
It used <a href="https://github.com/sachac/subed-record">subed-record</a> directives to define the output files and the visuals.
</p>

<p>
Then I used <code>subed-record</code> to start recording each segment. I pressed <code>&lt;left&gt;</code> (<code>subed-record-retry</code>) to re-record a segment as many times as I needed to. This adds or updates an <code>#+AUDIO</code> directive that points to the current recording and it also updates the timestamps. When I was satisfied, I pressed <code>&lt;right&gt;</code> (<code>subed-record-accept-segment</code>) to update the ending timestamp and move on to the next segment to record. Whenever I needed to take a break from recording, I used <code>RET</code> (<code>subed-record-accept-and-stop</code>) to accept the last segment and stop.
After recording, I used <a href="https://github.com/sachac/subed/blob/main/subed/subed-waveform.el">subed-waveform-show-all</a> to see the waveforms for each segment. I left- and right-clicked on the waveforms to refine the start and end timestamp, getting rid of the sound from the keyboard shortcuts, and I used <code>subed-split-subtitle</code> to split each long cue into the proper lines for the subtitles.
</p>

